{# The shell a client space is worked in.

   **Neither the back-office nor the public site, and that is deliberate.** A
   space is not a screen somebody passes through on the way to another one: it
   is a place they stay in for an hour, and the admin chrome around it costs
   what the work needs. The side menu alone is 260 pixels of a board whose
   columns are 288 - a whole column, given to a menu nobody uses while dragging
   a card.

   It is modelled on `@Studio/public/layout.html.twig`, which made the same
   choice for the same reason one screen earlier: a page addressed to one
   customer has no side menu to put a customer in.

   **The route lives under `/workspace`, and the firewall knows it.** The admin
   firewall is `^/(suite|dev|workspace)`: outside it no suite identity is
   restored, so a staff member would arrive as an anonymous visitor on their own
   client's board. A new prefix for these pages means a new entry in that
   pattern first - the trap that `suite_ged_files` exists to work around.

   The client reads the same space through a page of their own, on a public
   route reached by a signed link, with the layout this one was modelled on:
   two addresses, two ways of proving who you are, and one space behind them. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ app.request.locale }}">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        {# A client's content plan is nobody's business but theirs, and the page
           is behind a login either way; saying so costs nothing. #}
        <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
        <title>{% block title %}{{ space.name }}{% endblock %}</title>
        <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg">
        {{ include('@Shared/components/theme_script.html.twig', {colorScheme: true}) }}
        {{ vite_entry_link_tags('app') }}
        {{ include('@Shared/components/primary_color_style.html.twig') }}
        {# Privileges, flash messages and the colour presets. Without them a
           `usePrivileges()` call answers "no" to everything and the screen
           draws itself with every button missing. #}
        {{ include('@Shared/components/suite_globals.html.twig') }}
        {{ vite_entry_script_tags('app') }}
        {{ vite_entry_script_tags('theme') }}
        {# Every write on this screen reports through a toast, so the
           entry that mounts one has to be here as well as on the pages
           that inherit the admin layout. #}
        {{ vite_entry_script_tags('flash') }}

        {# **A screen that wants the whole window says so, and the shell follows.**

           A flex column only distributes the room it knows about: `flex-1`
           means `flex-basis: 0%`, and a percentage measured against an
           automatic height is treated as "the size of the content". A
           `min-height` on the body is therefore not enough - the page took
           back the height of what it contained, and the chat overflowed by
           sixty pixels, which is the worst amount: enough to scroll, not
           enough to be useful.

           The screen that wants to fit inside sets `data-fills-viewport`. The
           window then becomes a firm height, and the links of the column - the
           `main`, the mount div, the component's root - agree to shrink below
           the size of their content, which lets the thread scroll inside
           rather than the page outside.

           Nothing is hidden: on a really short screen, a phone lying on its
           side, the page scrolls again as before rather than cutting off the
           input field. #}
        <style>
            body:has([data-fills-viewport]) { height: 100dvh; }
            body:has([data-fills-viewport]) > main,
            body:has([data-fills-viewport]) > main > *,
            body:has([data-fills-viewport]) > main > * > * { min-height: 0; }
        </style>
    </head>
    {# **A column one screen high, and nothing more.** The chat is the only
       screen of a space that is read with nothing around it: it wants the room
       left under the header, exactly, so that the thread scrolls inside and
       the input field stays under the thumb. It got it by a subtraction - the
       window height minus eight and a half rem - which held for a header on
       one line; on a phone it takes two, and the sixty extra pixels scrolled
       the whole page by almost nothing, which is the worst amount: enough to
       move, not enough to be useful.

       The page now measures instead of guessing. The body is a column at
       least one window high, `main` takes what is left, and the screen that
       wants to fit inside says so with `flex-1`. Those taller than the window
       push as before: a flex item does not shrink below the size of its
       content.

       `dvh` and not `vh`: on a phone, `100vh` counts the address bar that is
       not there, and the page would run off the bottom of the screen. #}
    <body class="flex min-h-dvh flex-col bg-body transition-colors duration-200">
        {# The exit is explicit because the menu is gone. A page that removes
           the way back and offers none is a page people reach for the browser
           button on, which loses whatever they had open. #}
        <header class="sticky top-0 z-20 border-b border-line bg-surface/95 backdrop-blur">
            <div class="aurora-page-x flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-2 py-2 sm:gap-x-4 sm:py-2.5">
                {# **An arrow alone on a phone.** The label takes a hundred and
                   seventy-three pixels out of three hundred and seventy-five,
                   nearly half the bar for a word the chevron already says; the
                   space's name was left with a hundred and seventy. The text
                   comes back as soon as there is room, and it stays readable
                   by a screen reader in both cases. #}
                <a
                    href="{{ backPath }}"
                    class="-ml-2 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2 py-2 text-sm text-muted transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-primary sm:py-1"
                    aria-label="{{ 'suite.studio.space_content.back_to_spaces'|trans }}"
                >
                    <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                        <path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/>
                    </svg>
                    <span class="hidden sm:inline">
                        {{ 'suite.studio.space_content.back_to_spaces'|trans }}
                    </span>
                </a>

                <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center gap-2.5">
                    {# The same palette slot the space wears everywhere else, so
                       arriving here confirms which space was opened. #}
                    <span
                        class="h-2.5 w-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-full"
                        style="background-color: var(--chart-cat-{{ space.colourSlot }})"
                    ></span>
                    {# **The name opens the other spaces.** Moving from one
                       client to the next meant going back to the list every
                       time. A `<details>` rather than a script: it opens
                       without JavaScript and closes on a second click, and
                       picking a space leaves the page anyway. #}
                    {% set otherSpaces = studio_other_spaces(space.id, activeTab|default('content')) %}
                    {% if otherSpaces is empty %}
                        <div class="min-w-0">
                            <h1 class="truncate text-sm font-medium text-primary">{{ space.name }}</h1>
                            <p class="truncate text-xs text-muted">{{ space.customerName }}</p>
                        </div>
                    {% else %}
                        <details class="group relative min-w-0">
                            <summary
                                class="-mx-1.5 flex min-w-0 cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-1.5 py-0.5 transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
                                aria-label="{{ 'suite.studio.space_content.switch_space'|trans }}"
                            >
                                <span class="min-w-0">
                                    <h1 class="truncate text-sm font-medium text-primary">{{ space.name }}</h1>
                                    <span class="block truncate text-xs text-muted">{{ space.customerName }}</span>
                                </span>
                                <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 text-muted transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                                    <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/>
                                </svg>
                            </summary>
                            <div class="absolute left-0 z-30 mt-1 max-h-80 w-72 max-w-[calc(100vw-1.5rem)] overflow-y-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-1 shadow-lg">
                                <p class="px-2.5 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">{{ 'suite.studio.space_content.switch_space'|trans }}</p>
                                {% for other in otherSpaces %}
                                    <a href="{{ other.url }}" class="flex items-center gap-2.5 rounded-md px-2.5 py-1.5 transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2">
                                        <span class="h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full" style="background-color: var(--chart-cat-{{ other.colourSlot }})"></span>
                                        <span class="min-w-0">
                                            <span class="block truncate text-sm text-primary">{{ other.name }}</span>
                                            <span class="block truncate text-xs text-muted">{{ other.customerName }}</span>
                                        </span>
                                    </a>
                                {% endfor %}
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    {% endif %}
                    {% if space.archived %}
                        <span class="shrink-0 rounded-full bg-surface-2 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs text-muted">
                            {{ 'suite.studio.spaces.archived_badge'|trans }}
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}
                </div>

                {# Real links rather than a client-side switch: each view is a
                   place somebody sends a colleague to, and a URL that says
                   which one is open is what makes that possible. The back
                   button works for free. #}
                {# Full width on a phone, where it takes its own line anyway:
                   two finger targets are better than two words squeezed to the
                   left of an empty space. #}
                <nav
                    class="flex w-full shrink-0 items-center gap-1 sm:w-auto"
                    aria-label="{{ space.name }}"
                >
                    {# Two tabs, not four. The board, the list and the month are
                       three ways of reading the same rows, so they are a
                       selector inside Contenu rather than destinations beside
                       it: a reader should not have to know that one alternative
                       lives in a tab and another in a switch. #}
                    {% set tabs = [
                        {label: 'suite.studio.space_content.tab'|trans, url: boardPath, key: 'content'},
                    ] %}
                    {# The access page lists who the space is open to: the
                       same right that gives an access shows them. #}
                    {% if is_granted('studio.spaces.share') %}
                        {% set tabs = tabs|merge([{label: 'suite.studio.space_access.tab'|trans, url: accessPath, key: 'access'}]) %}
                    {% endif %}
                    {% for tab in tabs %}
                        {% if tab.key == activeTab|default('content') %}
                            <span class="flex-1 rounded-md bg-surface-2 px-2.5 py-1 text-center text-sm font-medium text-primary sm:flex-none sm:text-left" aria-current="page">
                                {{ tab.label }}
                            </span>
                        {% else %}
                            <a
                                href="{{ tab.url }}"
                                class="flex-1 rounded-md px-2.5 py-1 text-center text-sm text-muted transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-primary sm:flex-none sm:text-left"
                            >
                                {{ tab.label }}
                            </a>
                        {% endif %}
                    {% endfor %}
                </nav>
            </div>
        </header>

        {# **On a phone, a gutter just wide enough to be seen.**
           Sixteen pixels on each side out of three hundred and seventy-five is
           nearly a tenth of the width given to empty space, on the only device
           that has none to give. Eight are enough: the cards keep their
           rounded corners, so they read as blocks set down, and they win back
           sixteen pixels of content on the way.

           The rule holds for the rest of the application: the gutter shrinks
           on a phone, it does not disappear, and nothing changes in how the
           blocks are drawn. #}
        {# The back office's shared margin (`aurora-page`, spacing.css) rather
           than its own: hard-coded here, it was twenty-four pixels on the
           sides for sixteen at the top, and no gap between the blocks
           followed it. #}
        <main class="aurora-page flex flex-1 flex-col">
            {% block body %}{% endblock %}
        </main>
    </body>
</html>
